---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout'
---

<BaseLayout
    title='FOSS Alternative Finder'
    description='Find free and open-source alternatives to popular paid software — Photoshop, Office, Notion, Spotify and 60+ more, with licenses and platforms.'
>
    <h1 class='text-center text-4xl md:text-6xl font-semibold my-8'>
        FOSS Alternative Finder
    </h1>

    <p class='text-center text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 max-w-3xl mx-auto -mt-2 mb-8'>
        Paying for software you don't have to? Every alternative below is free, open source, and
        developed by a community — not a subscription department.
    </p>

    <div class='not-prose max-w-5xl mx-auto px-4'>
        <input
            id='f-search'
            type='search'
            placeholder='Search proprietary or open-source software, e.g. "photoshop"…'
            class='w-full rounded-lg border border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-700 bg-white dark:bg-[#0a0910] px-4 py-2.5'
        />

        <div class='flex flex-wrap justify-center items-center gap-2 my-4' id='f-cats'></div>

        <div class='flex flex-wrap justify-center items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-2 mb-6'>
            <label class='text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300'>
                Sort
                <select
                    id='f-sort'
                    class='ml-2 rounded-md border border-stone-300 dark:border-stone-700 bg-white dark:bg-[#0a0910] px-2 py-1'
                >
                    <option value='az'>A → Z (proprietary)</option>
                    <option value='za'>Z → A (proprietary)</option>
                    <option value='cat'>By category</option>
                </select>
            </label>
            <p id='f-count' class='text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400'></p>
        </div>

        <div id='alt-list' class='grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-2'></div>
    </div>

    <article class='prose prose-lg md:prose-xl dark:prose-invert min-w-full py-10'>
        <h2>About this tool</h2>
        <p>
            The FOSS Alternative Finder is a hand-curated mapping of popular paid and proprietary
            software to genuinely free and open-source replacements. Every alternative on this page
            ships under a recognized open-source license (MIT, GPL, Apache, MPL and similar), is
            developed by a community or a foundation, and costs nothing to use — for personal and,
            in almost all cases, commercial work too. Nothing here is sponsored: we list what we
            would actually install on our own machines.
        </p>

        <h2>How to use it</h2>
        <ol>
            <li>Type the name of the tool you are replacing — for example “photoshop” — in the search box.</li>
            <li>Narrow the list with the category chips, or switch the sort to browse by category.</li>
            <li>Click the green name to open the official project website and download it from there.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Always download open-source software from its official site. That is the only way to be
            sure you get the real, unmodified program.
        </p>

        <h2>Why choose open source at all?</h2>
        <p>
            Three practical reasons. First, cost: there is no subscription, no trial that expires,
            and no features locked behind a “pro” tier. Second, freedom: the license guarantees you
            can use, study, modify and share the software — it will not be taken away from you if a
            company changes its mind. Third, privacy and file formats: most open-source projects
            store your data in open standards, so you are never locked into one ecosystem.
        </p>

        <h2>Are open-source alternatives as good?</h2>
        <p>
            For everyday work — documents, photo editing, video cutting, notes, messaging, backups —
            yes, comfortably. Projects like LibreOffice, GIMP, Blender and VLC are used professionally
            every day and have been refined for decades. A fair warning: a few niche features of
            industry tools may be missing or work differently, so expect a short adjustment period.
            The good news is that trying costs you nothing but a download.
        </p>

        <h2>Can I run these on Windows or macOS?</h2>
        <p>
            Yes — the vast majority are cross-platform, and the badges on each card show where each
            one runs. A “Self-hosted” label means the software runs on your own server or computer
            and you connect to it from your browser or phone; that is easier than it sounds, and it
            means nobody else hosts your data.
        </p>

        <h2>Missing something?</h2>
        <p>
            This list grows over time. If your favorite open-source replacement is not here, or a
            pairing looks wrong, write to <a href='/contact-us/'>admin@getfoss.org</a> and tell us
            what to add.
        </p>
    </article>
</BaseLayout>

